    <p style="font-family:Calibri,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:16px;line-height:1.7;color:#232F3E;margin:0 0 14px 0;">I keep hearing the same phrase in conversations with payment operations leaders: <em>&#8220;We&#8217;ll start with hybrid. It&#8217;s cheaper.&#8221;</em></p>

    <p style="font-family:Calibri,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:16px;line-height:1.7;color:#232F3E;margin:0 0 14px 0;">It isn&#8217;t.</p>

    <p style="font-family:Calibri,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:16px;line-height:1.7;color:#232F3E;margin:0 0 14px 0;">I understand the instinct. When you&#8217;re facing the November 2026 deadline and budgets are tight, &#8220;minimum compliance&#8221; sounds like prudent cost management. Do the least you need to. Hit the regulatory floor. Move on. Except here&#8217;s what that logic misses: <strong style="font-weight:700;">hybrid and structured addressing require identical implementation effort.</strong></p>

    <p style="font-family:Calibri,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:16px;line-height:1.7;color:#232F3E;margin:0 0 14px 0;">Both deploy via sidecar architecture — sitting alongside your core payment systems, not replacing them. Same integration. Same 10&#8211;16 week timeline. Same project. No core system replacement for either approach. The only difference is what you get out of it.</p>

    <p style="font-family:Calibri,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:16px;line-height:1.7;color:#232F3E;margin:0 0 14px 0;">Hybrid-only compliance delivers a marginal straight-through processing improvement: perhaps 5&#8211;15%. Structured address resolution takes STP from the industry average of approximately 40% to above 98%. That&#8217;s not an incremental gain. It&#8217;s a transformation — and it costs the same to implement.</p>

    <p style="font-family:Calibri,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:16px;line-height:1.7;color:#232F3E;margin:0 0 14px 0;">So &#8220;minimum compliance&#8221; doesn&#8217;t save money. It means paying full price for a fraction of the value. And the fraction you leave behind? That&#8217;s the $8&#8211;12 billion problem the industry is bleeding through every year.</p>

    <p style="font-family:Calibri,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:16px;line-height:1.7;color:#232F3E;margin:0 0 20px 0;">Let&#8217;s break down what that actually costs your institution.</p>

